// Hard digital-SAT R&W bank questions (craft). Auto-generated; verified content.
window.SAT_DATA = window.SAT_DATA || {};
(window.SAT_DATA.rwBank = window.SAT_DATA.rwBank || []).push(
{"id": "rw-wic-a1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Measurements by the Cassini spacecraft revealed that the plumes erupting from the south pole of Saturn's moon Enceladus contain molecular hydrogen, a compound that certain microbes on Earth consume as an energy source. Mission scientists, however, were careful to ______ the announcement of this discovery: the presence of a potential energy source, they cautioned, establishes only that the moon's subsurface ocean could support life, not that it actually does."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "amplify", "why": "Reversed: the colon shows the scientists restricting the claim's scope (\"establishes only that... could support life\"), not strengthening or broadening the announcement.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "retract", "why": "Too strong for the context: the scientists still stand behind the hydrogen detection itself; they attach a caution to it, but nothing suggests they withdrew the announcement.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "qualify", "why": "In this context \"qualify\" means to limit or moderate a statement: the colon supplies the exact limitation the scientists attached—the finding \"establishes only that the moon's subsurface ocean could support life, not that it actually does.\""}, {"text": "reiterate", "why": "Merely repeating the announcement would not capture the cautionary restriction introduced after the colon; the scientists are narrowing the claim, not restating it.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-wic-a2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from Charles Dickens's 1861 novel Great Expectations. The narrator describes Mr. Jaggers, a London lawyer.\n\nHowever freely his clients confided in him, Mr. Jaggers himself remained close: he would hear out the whole of a man's history with perfect composure and offer in return neither opinion nor promise, so that even those who had trusted him with their affairs for years could not have said what he thought on any matter."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"close\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Uncommunicative", "why": "The colon defines the word: Jaggers offers \"neither opinion nor promise,\" and even longtime clients \"could not have said what he thought\"—reticence, a secondary sense of \"close.\""}, {"text": "Attentive", "why": "Jaggers does listen carefully (\"hear out the whole of a man's history\"), but that detail concerns how he receives confidences, not what \"close\" means; the colon glosses the word as withholding, not paying attention.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Intimate", "why": "\"Close\" can describe intimacy, but the passage stresses the opposite dynamic: clients confide everything while Jaggers reveals nothing, so the word marks distance, not closeness of relationship.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Nearby", "why": "This is the most common dictionary meaning of \"close,\" but nothing in the text concerns physical proximity.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-wic-a3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In 2020, a team of astronomers reported spectroscopic evidence of phosphine—a gas that, on Earth, is produced almost exclusively by microbes and by industrial processes—in the clouds of Venus. Subsequent reanalyses have rendered the original detection ______: independent groups processing the same raw data have variously recovered a much weaker signal, no signal at all, or a feature better explained by ordinary sulfur dioxide."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "definitive", "why": "Reversed: the reanalyses' conflicting outcomes (weaker signal, no signal, an alternative molecule) undermine certainty about the detection rather than settle the question.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "untenable", "why": "Too strong: some reprocessings still recover a weakened phosphine signal, so the detection has been thrown into doubt, not shown to be indefensible.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "immaterial", "why": "The reanalyses bear on whether the detection is real, not on whether it matters; nothing suggests a possible biosignature on Venus became unimportant.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "equivocal", "why": "\"Equivocal\" (open to more than one interpretation) matches the evidence after the colon: the same raw data yield \"a much weaker signal, no signal at all, or a feature better explained by ordinary sulfur dioxide.\""}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-wic-a4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Because Mercury lacks an atmosphere substantial enough to burn up incoming debris and has experienced comparatively little resurfacing by volcanism or erosion, its terrain preserves a nearly complete record of past impacts. Planetary geologists accordingly treat crater density as a straightforward ______ of a surface's age: the more craters a given region has accumulated, the longer that region has been exposed to bombardment."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "byproduct", "why": "Craters do accumulate as a consequence of long exposure, but the sentence describes an inferential use—geologists \"treat\" density as a way to read a surface's age—so a word meaning incidental result misstates the relationship.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "index", "why": "In this context an \"index\" is an indicator or measure: the colon spells out the correlation (\"the more craters..., the longer... exposed\") that lets geologists infer a surface's age from its crater counts."}, {"text": "determinant", "why": "Reverses the causal direction: a surface's crater density does not determine its age; longer exposure produces more craters, and geologists merely infer age from them.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "relic", "why": "Individual craters might be called relics of ancient impacts, but crater density—a statistical measure—is not itself a surviving remnant of anything.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-wic-a5", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In the 1880s and 1890s, administrators at the Harvard College Observatory often characterized the work of the women employed there as \"computers\" as essentially ______: classifying stellar spectra was, in their telling, repetitive labor that demanded diligence rather than insight. Yet it was precisely this supposedly routine cataloging that enabled Annie Jump Cannon to devise the spectral classification scheme that astronomers still use today."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "esoteric", "why": "Reversed: work said to demand \"diligence rather than insight\" is being cast as ordinary, not as requiring rare or specialized understanding.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "superfluous", "why": "The administrators belittled the work as routine, not as unnecessary—the observatory depended on the computers' cataloging, and nothing in the text calls the labor expendable.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "quotidian", "why": "\"Quotidian\" (ordinary, everyday) matches the colon's gloss—\"repetitive labor that demanded diligence rather than insight\"—and is echoed by the later phrase \"supposedly routine cataloging.\""}, {"text": "ephemeral", "why": "Nothing in the text concerns the work's duration or transience; the final sentence in fact stresses the lasting value of the cataloging.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-wic-b1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Among many songbirds, the vigor of a male's dawn song signals his physical condition to prospective mates. Ecologist Priya Raman found that persistent noise from a nearby highway did not silence the birds but ______ their songs: males near the road sang at reduced amplitude and with fewer repetitions, blunting the very signal on which females rely."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "amplified", "why": "Means increased in strength or volume, the opposite of the described reduction in \"amplitude\" and \"repetitions.\"", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "silenced", "why": "The text states the noise \"did not silence the birds,\" so this directly contradicts the passage; the birds still sang, only more weakly.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "attenuated", "why": "Means weakened or reduced without eliminating; precisely matches songs sung at \"reduced amplitude and with fewer repetitions\" that merely \"blunt\" the signal rather than remove it."}, {"text": "diversified", "why": "Means made more varied, which contradicts \"fewer repetitions\"; the songs became simpler and weaker, not more varied.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-wic-b2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Behavioral economist Lucía Herrera studies why so many people save too little for retirement. Her experiments suggest that people systematically ______ future rewards, valuing a payment available today far more than an identical payment promised years later—a bias that no amount of financial-literacy training in her trials fully corrected."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "discount", "why": "In economics, to discount is to assign less weight or value to; this captures exactly the described tendency to value a payment today \"far more than an identical payment promised years later.\""}, {"text": "overstate", "why": "Means to exaggerate the value of, the reverse of the described behavior: people undervalue, not overvalue, future rewards.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "anticipate", "why": "Means to expect or look forward to; it fails to convey the reduced valuation the sentence spells out, since anticipating a reward does not mean valuing it less.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "itemize", "why": "Means to list individually, which has nothing to do with how people weigh present against future payments.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-wic-b3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When sea otters were reintroduced to a stretch of the Aleutian coast, populations of sea urchins that had overgrazed the region's kelp forests were quickly ______: the otters' heavy predation kept urchin numbers low enough that the kelp could regrow and stabilize."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "monitored", "why": "Means merely observed or tracked; the otters actively suppressed the urchins through predation rather than watching them, and observation would not let the kelp recover.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "catalogued", "why": "Means recorded or listed in an inventory, which does not fit an outcome in which urchin numbers were physically driven down.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "sustained", "why": "Means kept going or maintained, the reverse of what happened: urchin numbers were reduced, not held up.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "checked", "why": "Uses the sense \"held in restraint, curbed\"; precisely matches predation that \"kept urchin numbers low\" so the kelp could recover."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-wic-b4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When two economic variables rise and fall together, researchers caution, the correlation between them may be ______: an unmeasured third factor—a booming regional economy, say—can drive up both ice cream sales and home prices at once, without either one causing the other."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "deliberate", "why": "Means intentional; correlations between economic variables are not planned, and intention is irrelevant to the point about an unmeasured cause.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "spurious", "why": "Means falsely suggesting a real relationship; matches a correlation produced by a hidden third factor \"without either one causing the other.\""}, {"text": "negligible", "why": "Means so small as to be unimportant, but the variables demonstrably \"rise and fall together,\" so the correlation is sizable; the flaw is its lack of causation, not its magnitude.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "reciprocal", "why": "Means mutual or two-way, implying each variable affects the other, which the text explicitly rules out with \"without either one causing the other.\"", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-wic-b5", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Studying a population of New Caledonian crows, ecologist Tomás Rivera found that the birds' foraging behavior is remarkably ______: individuals readily abandon a once-productive technique and adopt an entirely new one when the local supply of prey shifts, rather than repeating a fixed routine regardless of conditions."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "fragile", "why": "Means easily broken or delicate, which says nothing about the birds' capacity to switch techniques as conditions change.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "synthetic", "why": "Invokes the common sense of \"plastic\" as artificial material; the required meaning concerns adaptability of behavior, not artificiality.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "plastic", "why": "In biology, plastic means readily modified or adaptable; matches crows that \"abandon\" one technique and \"adopt an entirely new one\" as prey shifts."}, {"text": "instinctive", "why": "Means innate and fixed, the reverse of behavior described as flexibly changing rather than following \"a fixed routine.\"", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-wic-c1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Attribution debates over an unsigned fifteenth-century Netherlandish altarpiece have increasingly turned on infrared reflectography, an imaging technique that exposes the underdrawing beneath a painting's surface. Yet the evidence such imaging yields is often ______: the hesitant, heavily reworked contour lines that some scholars read as the labored copying of a workshop assistant could just as plausibly reflect the master's own habit of revising a composition even as he painted it."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "conclusive", "why": "Reversed: the colon shows that the same contour lines support two rival attributions, so the evidence settles nothing—the opposite of conclusive.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "extraneous", "why": "The imaging is central to the dispute—the debates 'have increasingly turned on' it—so the evidence cannot be beside the point.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "fabricated", "why": "No one in the passage doubts that the underdrawings are genuine; the difficulty lies in interpreting them, so 'fabricated' overstates the problem.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "equivocal", "why": "Correct: the colon explains the blank—identical lines 'could just as plausibly' support either the assistant or the master, so the evidence admits more than one interpretation, i.e., is equivocal."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-wic-c2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Obsidian forms when silica-rich lava is quenched so rapidly—by contact with air, water, or cold surrounding rock—that its atoms have no time to organize themselves into a crystalline lattice. Whereas crystals in a slowly cooling magma chamber may nucleate and grow over centuries, in an obsidian flow that growth is arrested within hours, producing a natural glass whose disordered structure preserves a chemical record of the original melt."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"arrested\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Halted", "why": "Correct: atoms 'have no time to organize' into crystals and the product is a crystal-free glass, so growth is stopped outright—the pointed contrast with centuries of growth in a slow-cooling magma chamber."}, {"text": "Captivated", "why": "'Arresting' can mean attention-grabbing, but that sense applies to observers, not to a physical process; crystal growth cannot be captivated.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Apprehended", "why": "This is the law-enforcement sense of 'arrest'; nothing in the passage is seized or taken into custody.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Slowed", "why": "Intensity mismatch: because the result is a glass with no crystalline lattice at all, growth does not merely proceed more slowly—it ceases entirely.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-wic-c3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Musicologist Renata Okafor does not deny that the phonograph reshaped domestic musical life in the early twentieth century, but she seeks to ______ the familiar claim that recorded sound doomed amateur music-making: recordings did displace some parlor performance, she concedes, yet sheet-music sales had begun falling decades before records became affordable, and community choral societies actually multiplied during the height of the record boom."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "refute", "why": "Too strong: Okafor 'does not deny' the phonograph's impact and concedes that recordings displaced some performance, so she is limiting the claim, not proving it wholly false.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "reiterate", "why": "She is not repeating the familiar claim; everything after the colon pushes back against its strongest version.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "qualify", "why": "Correct: in its secondary sense, 'qualify' means to limit or moderate a claim. Okafor concedes part of the claim's substance but adds evidence—the earlier sheet-music decline, multiplying choral societies—that restricts its scope."}, {"text": "corroborate", "why": "Reversed: her evidence complicates the claim rather than confirming it.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-wic-c4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In steep terrain, the kinetic energy of falling raindrops is a principal driver of soil erosion. Field experiments in Puerto Rico's Luquillo Mountains suggest that a dense forest canopy can ______ this erosive force: drops intercepted by foliage coalesce and fall again from leaf tips, reaching the soil with much of their original momentum spent. The protection is partial rather than complete—plots beneath the canopy still lost soil, only more slowly than exposed plots did."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "nullify", "why": "Too strong: the passage insists the protection is 'partial rather than complete' and that canopied plots still lost soil, so the force is not canceled.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "attenuate", "why": "Correct: to attenuate is to weaken in force or intensity—intercepted drops arrive 'with much of their original momentum spent,' and erosion continues 'only more slowly,' i.e., the force is reduced but not eliminated."}, {"text": "precipitate", "why": "Despite its superficially apt rain-related ring, 'precipitate' means to bring about or hasten—the opposite of the canopy's effect on erosion.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "supplant", "why": "Nothing replaces the raindrops' force; the drops still strike the soil, only with diminished momentum.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-wic-c5", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In the lithographs of her final decade, the German artist Käthe Kollwitz abandoned the dense crosshatching of her early prints, trusting a few charcoal-dark strokes to carry an entire composition. Critics have long admired the economy of these late images: in Seed for Sowing Must Not Be Ground (1941), a single sweeping contour describes the mother's sheltering arm, and that one gesture registers protection, defiance, and exhaustion all at once."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"economy\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Prosperity", "why": "This is the financial sense of 'economy'; the text concerns artistic means, not wealth or markets.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Thrift", "why": "Thrift is the sparing management of money or material resources; the sentence praises what a single stroke expressively accomplishes, not careful saving.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Efficiency", "why": "Correct: the colon defines the term—'a single sweeping contour' makes one gesture register 'protection, defiance, and exhaustion all at once,' maximal expressive effect achieved with minimal means."}, {"text": "Moderation", "why": "Moderation implies restraint or the avoidance of extremes, but the images are emotionally intense; the praise concerns how much one stroke achieves, not how restrained it is.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-tsp-a1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Bulk metallic glasses—alloys cooled so rapidly that their atoms freeze in a disordered, liquid-like arrangement—can be twice as strong as conventional titanium alloys, and their lack of grain boundaries confers exceptional resistance to corrosion and wear. [[u]]That same disorder, however, deprives a yielding metallic glass of any internal structure that might arrest a propagating shear band, so failure, when it comes, is sudden and catastrophic.[[/u]] Materials engineer Priya Raghunathan's team has therefore embedded soft crystalline dendrites within the glassy matrix, producing composites that sacrifice a small fraction of the alloys' strength but blunt nascent shear bands before they can spread."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It explains the process by which rapid cooling produces the disordered atomic arrangement characteristic of bulk metallic glasses.", "why": "The cooling process is explained in the first sentence's parenthetical definition; the underlined sentence takes that arrangement as given and describes its consequence for failure, not its origin.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "It disputes the claim that the disordered atomic structure of bulk metallic glasses is responsible for the materials' unusual strength.", "why": "The underlined sentence accepts the strength claim and adds a downside of the same disorder ('That same disorder, however'); it qualifies the praise rather than disputing it, so this reverses a concession into a refutation.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It identifies a serious drawback arising from the very feature that makes the materials attractive, thereby establishing the problem that the engineering strategy in the next sentence addresses.", "why": "'That same disorder, however' ties the flaw to the previously praised structure, and 'has therefore embedded' shows the final sentence responding directly to the problem the underlined sentence raises."}, {"text": "It predicts that Raghunathan's dendrite-reinforced composites will ultimately fail as suddenly as unmodified metallic glasses do.", "why": "The underlined sentence describes unmodified glasses and precedes any mention of the composites; the text says the composites 'blunt nascent shear bands,' making no prediction of their failure.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-tsp-a2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Critics have long read Emily Dickinson's irregular dashes as marks of hesitation—typographic stutters registering a mind at odds with itself. Examining the fascicles, the hand-sewn booklets into which Dickinson copied finished poems, literary scholar Marta Osei finds that the dashes in these fair copies are frequently more numerous, and more varied in length and angle, than those in the rough drafts from which they were copied. If the dashes were symptoms of compositional uncertainty, Osei reasons, revision should have pruned them; that Dickinson instead multiplied them suggests a deliberate notation—one that may score the poems for pace and breath."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "To present manuscript evidence that complicates a long-standing critical interpretation of a signature feature of Dickinson's poems.", "why": "The text opens with the received reading of the dashes as 'marks of hesitation,' then deploys Osei's fascicle findings to argue the marks were 'a deliberate notation'—evidence marshaled against the prevailing interpretation."}, {"text": "To argue that Dickinson's rough drafts reveal more about her compositional habits than her carefully prepared fair copies do.", "why": "Osei's inference rests on what the fair copies show (more, and more varied, dashes); the text never ranks the drafts above the fair copies as evidence, so this reverses the direction of the comparison.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "To describe the method by which Dickinson gathered her finished poems into hand-sewn fascicles.", "why": "The fascicles are defined in a brief appositive purely as background for Osei's evidence; elevating this detail to the text's purpose ignores the argument about what the dashes mean.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "To demonstrate that critics have overstated how frequently dashes appear in Dickinson's poetry.", "why": "The critical dispute concerns what the dashes signify, not how often they occur—and Osei's finding is that Dickinson made the dashes more numerous, not that critics inflated their count.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-tsp-a3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Nacre, the iridescent lining of some mollusk shells, is roughly 95 percent aragonite, a brittle mineral, yet the composite is thousands of times tougher than aragonite alone; the standard explanation credits nacre's \"brick and mortar\" arrangement of mineral platelets bound by elastic biopolymers, which deflects growing cracks along tortuous paths. When engineer Lucía Ferrandis's team fabricated synthetic replicas that reproduced this architecture with high fidelity, however, the replicas achieved only a fraction of nacre's toughness. The team now suspects that nanoscale asperities—minute bumps that interlock neighboring platelets under stress—contribute more to the material's performance than the celebrated architecture itself."}, "stem": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "It describes a natural composite, outlines the process used to manufacture synthetic versions of it, and assesses those versions' commercial potential.", "why": "The text never assesses commercial potential, and the replicas matter only as a test of the standard explanation; this misses the text's argumentative arc entirely.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It presents an explanation for a material's properties, reports an experiment that confirmed that explanation, and recommends directions for future research.", "why": "The experiment did the opposite of confirming: replicas with the same architecture 'achieved only a fraction of nacre's toughness,' which is why the team revised its view.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It identifies a puzzling property of a natural material and then presents two competing explanations that the author judges equally credible.", "why": "The two explanations are not left balanced: the replica result undercuts the architecture-based account, and the final sentence reports the team's shift toward the asperity hypothesis.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It states the accepted explanation for a material's unusual toughness, describes an experimental result that the explanation cannot fully account for, and introduces a revised hypothesis prompted by that result.", "why": "The sequence is exact: 'the standard explanation credits' the brick-and-mortar architecture; the high-fidelity replicas nonetheless underperformed ('however'); and 'The team now suspects' nanoscale asperities—a hypothesis prompted by the shortfall."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-tsp-a4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Poet Agha Shahid Ali insisted that English-language ghazals honor the form's constitutive rules: autonomous couplets, a recurring end-refrain (the radif), and the rhyme that immediately precedes it. Some contemporary poets treat these requirements as ornamental, contending that the couplets' self-sufficiency alone conveys the form's spirit. [[u]]It is true that a refrain repeated across a dozen couplets can, in unskilled hands, deaden into mere echo.[[/u]] But the radif's insistent return is precisely what renders each couplet's swerve in subject legible as a swerve; to abandon the refrain is to produce not a freer ghazal but a sequence of disconnected aphorisms."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It provides an example of a contemporary English-language ghazal whose refrain has lapsed into monotony.", "why": "The sentence describes a hypothetical risk ('can, in unskilled hands') and names no poem or poet; no example is ever given in the text.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It concedes a genuine risk in the formal requirement the text defends, lending force to the counterargument that follows by showing the opposing view has been taken seriously.", "why": "'It is true that' grants the dissenting poets a real point about the refrain, and the immediate pivot 'But the radif's insistent return is precisely what...' rebuts them—a concession positioned to strengthen the rebuttal."}, {"text": "It explains why Agha Shahid Ali regarded the couplets of a ghazal as autonomous.", "why": "Couplet autonomy is listed among the form's rules in the first sentence and invoked by the dissenting poets in the second; the underlined sentence concerns the refrain, not why couplets stand alone.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "It signals that the author ultimately sides with the poets who treat the ghazal's formal rules as ornamental.", "why": "The very next sentence argues that abandoning the refrain yields 'not a freer ghazal but a sequence of disconnected aphorisms'; the author concedes a point only to reject the ornamental view, so this reverses the text's stance.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tsp-b1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Marine biologist Priya Anand and her colleagues have proposed that the Hawaiian bobtail squid recruits luminescent Vibrio fischeri bacteria into a specialized light organ chiefly to camouflage the squid's silhouette against moonlit water, a strategy known as counterillumination. [[u]]Skeptics might counter that the bacteria simply exploit a convenient habitat, conferring no benefit on their host.[[/u]] But Anand's team found that squid deprived of the bacteria were captured by predators far more often than colonized squid were, indicating that the partnership is not merely incidental to the squid but actively protective."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It summarizes the central conclusion that Anand's team designed their experiment to establish.", "why": "The underlined sentence states the skeptics' position, which is the opposite of the conclusion Anand's team reaches; the closing sentence, not this one, states the team's conclusion.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It anticipates a competing explanation in order to show that it is ultimately compatible with Anand's hypothesis.", "why": "The word 'But' opening the next sentence signals that the team's evidence contradicts this objection, not that the two views are compatible.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It raises an alternative interpretation of the squid-bacteria relationship that the passage's concluding evidence is meant to counter.", "why": "The sentence voices a rival reading (the bacteria merely 'exploit a convenient habitat'), and the following 'But Anand's team found...' supplies evidence offered to counter it."}, {"text": "It concedes a weakness in Anand's experimental design that qualifies the strength of her team's conclusion.", "why": "The objection concerns how to interpret the symbiosis, not any weakness in the study's methods; the passage treats the conclusion as strengthened, not qualified.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-tsp-b2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When the city of Rasmussen introduced a congestion charge on vehicles entering its downtown core, transit planners predicted that traffic would simply divert to untolled peripheral streets, worsening conditions in adjacent neighborhoods. Yet in the two years after the charge took effect, economist Dolores Vance found that traffic volumes fell not only downtown but on those peripheral streets as well, as many commuters abandoned driving altogether in favor of expanded bus service. Vance concludes that the charge's success depended less on the toll itself than on the transit improvements the toll revenue financed."}, "stem": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "It presents a prediction about a policy's consequences, reports evidence that runs counter to the prediction, and then offers an account of why the policy succeeded.", "why": "The text moves from the planners' prediction (traffic would divert), to a contrary finding (volumes fell everywhere), to Vance's explanation (the transit improvements financed by the toll)."}, {"text": "It confirms transit planners' forecast about a policy and then recommends extending the policy to nearby neighborhoods.", "why": "The finding contradicted the planners' forecast rather than confirming it, and the text makes no recommendation about extending the policy.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It contrasts two rival explanations for a decline in downtown traffic and then endorses one of them.", "why": "This captures only the final sentence's toll-versus-transit contrast and ignores the prediction and the surprising finding that structure the passage as a whole.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It describes an unexpected outcome of a policy and then concludes that the outcome defies existing traffic models.", "why": "Vance does explain the outcome, crediting the transit improvements, so the text does not conclude that it defies existing models.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-tsp-b3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Biologists long assumed that the elaborate, energetically costly migrations of leatherback sea turtles across entire ocean basins served solely to bring the turtles to reliable feeding grounds. Marine ecologist Tomas Ferrar, however, has documented that many leatherbacks bypass rich foraging zones to reach cooler waters where jellyfish, though sparser, contain fewer of the toxins the turtles struggle to metabolize. Ferrar proposes that the migrations are shaped less by the abundance of prey than by its quality."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "To detail the metabolic difficulty that toxins in jellyfish pose for leatherback sea turtles.", "why": "The turtles' difficulty metabolizing toxins is a supporting detail within Ferrar's argument, not the passage's overall purpose.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "To establish that leatherback sea turtles migrate chiefly to reach the ocean's most abundant sources of prey.", "why": "Ferrar's argument rejects prey abundance as the main driver, favoring prey quality instead, so this reverses his point.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "To compare the migratory routes of leatherback sea turtles with those of other ocean-crossing species.", "why": "No other migratory species is mentioned; the text discusses only leatherbacks.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "To present a researcher's revised account of the purpose of a migration that had been explained differently.", "why": "The passage opens with the long-standing 'feeding grounds' assumption and then advances Ferrar's competing account that quality, not abundance, shapes the migration."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-tsp-b4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When cities replace curbside parking with protected bike lanes, local merchants frequently protest that the loss of parking will drive away customers and depress sales. Urban researcher Nadia Okonkwo analyzed transaction data from twelve commercial corridors before and after such conversions and found that retail revenue rose, on average, in the year following installation. [[u]]Okonkwo cautions, however, that her corridors were all in dense, transit-rich districts where few shoppers arrived by car to begin with.[[/u]] Whether the same gains would materialize in automobile-dependent suburbs, she notes, remains an open question."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It rebuts merchants' concerns about parking by showing that the transaction data do not bear those concerns out.", "why": "Rebutting the merchants' concern is the work of the preceding sentence (the revenue finding); the underlined sentence instead limits how far that finding reaches.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It restricts the reach of Okonkwo's finding by pointing to a feature of the studied corridors that may keep the results from applying elsewhere.", "why": "The sentence notes that the corridors were all 'dense, transit-rich' with few car-borne shoppers, restricting how far the finding generalizes, a point the next sentence extends to suburbs."}, {"text": "It concedes that a flaw in Okonkwo's data undermines her conclusion that retail revenue increased.", "why": "Okonkwo does not doubt that revenue rose in her corridors; she questions only whether the result would hold elsewhere, which concerns scope, not a flaw in the data.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It anticipates an objection to bike lanes only to dismiss it as inconsistent with the evidence she gathered.", "why": "The sentence concedes a limitation rather than dismissing an objection; nothing in it is dismissed as inconsistent with the evidence.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tsp-c1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When Vaslav Nijinsky's choreography for The Rite of Spring vanished after only a handful of 1913 performances, scholars assumed the work was irrecoverable. In the 1980s, however, dance historian Millicent Hodson pieced together the choreography from an eclectic archive: a rehearsal director's annotated score, sketches by an audience member, and the fading recollections of surviving dancers. [[u]]Hodson has never claimed that her 1987 reconstruction reproduces Nijinsky's original with perfect fidelity.[[/u]] Yet by anchoring each inference to a documented source, she produced a version that most critics accept as broadly faithful to the ballet's radical design."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It emphasizes that Hodson regarded her reconstruction as a definitive recovery of Nijinsky's lost choreography.", "why": "Reverses the sentence's meaning: it states that Hodson never claimed perfect fidelity, so it cannot present her as treating the reconstruction as definitive.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It concedes a limitation of the reconstruction that the text then argues does not diminish the work's scholarly value.", "why": "The sentence admits the reconstruction is not a perfect copy, and the following sentence ('Yet by anchoring each inference...') argues it is nonetheless broadly faithful, so the sentence concedes a limitation the text goes on to rebut."}, {"text": "It catalogs the specific archival sources Hodson relied on to recover the choreography.", "why": "The archival sources are itemized in the preceding sentence, not the underlined one; this describes a different sentence's function.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "It indicates that critics have largely dismissed Hodson's reconstruction as inaccurate.", "why": "Contradicts the final sentence, which states that most critics accept the reconstruction as broadly faithful.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tsp-c2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "For centuries, cooks across Mesoamerica soaked and simmered dried maize in an alkaline solution of water and mineral lime or wood ash—a process now called nixtamalization—before grinding the softened kernels into dough. Early European observers dismissed the step as a mere matter of taste or texture. Only in the twentieth century did nutritional chemists determine that the alkaline treatment releases niacin locked within the kernel, a form of vitamin B3 the body otherwise cannot absorb. Populations that adopted maize as a dietary staple without the technique, by contrast, frequently suffered from pellagra, a debilitating niacin-deficiency disease."}, "stem": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "It traces the gradual spread of maize cultivation from Mesoamerica to other regions of the world.", "why": "The text never follows maize's geographic diffusion; it stays focused on a single preparation step and its nutritional effect.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It describes a modern scientific discovery and then recounts the traditional practice that researchers devised to apply it.", "why": "Reverses the chronology: the practice is centuries old and the chemical explanation came later, so researchers did not devise the practice to apply a discovery.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It contrasts two rival techniques for grinding maize and evaluates the nutritional merits of each.", "why": "Only one technique is discussed, set against its absence rather than a competing method, and grinding is not the passage's focus.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It presents a long-standing practice that observers once undervalued and then explains the nutritional function that accounts for its importance.", "why": "The passage opens with a practice European observers 'dismissed' and then explains that nixtamalization releases otherwise-unabsorbable niacin, the function that establishes its importance."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-tsp-c3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The Living Newspaper, a dramatic form developed by the U.S. Federal Theatre Project in the 1930s, staged current events using documentary techniques: performers recited verbatim from congressional records, newspapers, and court transcripts while projected headlines and a booming loudspeaker framed the action. Productions such as One-Third of a Nation dramatized housing shortages and farm crises not by inventing sympathetic characters but by marshaling statistics and official pronouncements into rapid, montage-like scenes. The form's creators wagered that audiences confronted with the documentary record itself would be moved more forcefully than audiences offered a conventional plot."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "To describe a theatrical form and the documentary strategy through which its creators sought to move audiences.", "why": "The text lays out the Living Newspaper's documentary techniques and its creators' aim of moving audiences 'more forcefully than' conventional plots do, pairing the form with the strategy behind it."}, {"text": "To analyze the political controversy that One-Third of a Nation provoked among government officials.", "why": "The production is named only as an example of the form's methods; the text mentions no controversy it provoked.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "To argue that documentary theater is inherently more persuasive than drama built around fictional characters.", "why": "The text attributes that belief to the form's creators ('The form's creators wagered'); it reports their view rather than advancing the general claim in its own voice.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "To trace the influence of the Living Newspaper on later forms of political theater.", "why": "The passage describes the form in the 1930s but never follows its effect on subsequent drama.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-tsp-c4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Chocolate's characteristic flavor does not reside in the raw cacao seed; it emerges only after harvested seeds, still cloaked in their sugary pulp, are heaped in boxes or beneath banana leaves to ferment. Agricultural scientist Rosa Delgado and her colleagues sampled the microbial communities at successive stages of this fermentation. [[u]]Batches dominated early by certain yeast species consistently developed the fruity, floral notes prized by chocolate makers, whereas batches quickly overtaken by acetic-acid bacteria turned harsh and sour.[[/u]] Delgado's team concluded that governing which microbes prevail—rather than the cacao variety alone—gives producers a practical lever for shaping flavor."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It explains why the raw cacao seed lacks the flavor associated with finished chocolate.", "why": "That point is made in the opening sentence; the underlined portion instead reports fermentation outcomes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It identifies a flaw in Delgado's method of sampling the fermenting microbial communities.", "why": "No methodological shortcoming is raised; the sampling is described neutrally in the preceding sentence.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It reports the contrasting results that support the team's conclusion about how flavor can be controlled.", "why": "The sentence sets the fruity yeast-led batches against the sour bacteria-led ones, supplying the evidence for the team's conclusion that governing microbes shapes flavor."}, {"text": "It recommends a specific fermentation procedure for producers to adopt.", "why": "The underlined portion presents results, not a recommendation; the practical implication appears in the final sentence and names no particular procedure.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ctc-a1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Educators typically assume that learning proceeds best when material is presented as clearly as possible. Psychologist Mara Ellison's experiments suggest otherwise: students who studied science passages printed in a small, slightly blurred font later recalled significantly more of the content than students who read identical passages in a crisp, familiar font. Ellison argues that processing difficulty is itself the active ingredient, forcing readers into the deeper, more effortful engagement on which durable memory depends.", "text2": "Cognitive scientist Priya Raghunathan tested hard-to-read fonts in experiments involving more than 2,000 students and found that the fonts improved recall only when participants could study for as long as they wished; when study time was fixed, the benefit vanished. Raghunathan also observed that participants reading difficult fonts under untimed conditions lingered measurably longer over each passage. In her view, such fonts operate not by deepening engagement but by inducing readers to allot more time to the material."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Raghunathan (Text 2) most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By denying that hard-to-read fonts can improve students' recall under any experimental conditions", "why": "Overstates Raghunathan's position: her own experiments showed that difficult fonts did improve recall when participants could study for as long as they wished.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "By observing that readers who spend additional time with a passage typically come to engage with it more deeply", "why": "Reverses Text 2's key contrast: Raghunathan explicitly separates added time from deepened engagement, holding that the fonts operate 'not by deepening engagement' but by prompting readers to spend more time.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By contending that the recall advantage Ellison observed may reflect the additional study time that difficult fonts prompt rather than a deeper mode of engagement with the material", "why": "Text 2 reports that the fonts' benefit disappeared when study time was fixed and that untimed readers 'lingered measurably longer,' leading Raghunathan to conclude the fonts induce readers 'to allot more time to the material'—a direct alternative to Ellison's engagement mechanism."}, {"text": "By pointing out that Ellison neglected to measure how long her participants spent studying each passage", "why": "Text 2 describes Raghunathan's own measurements and timing conditions; it provides no information about Ellison's methods, so this critique has no basis in the texts.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ctc-a2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Brutalist buildings of the 1950s through the 1970s, with their exposed concrete, massive volumes, and relentlessly repeated modules, routinely top surveys of the public's least-loved architecture. Critic Daniel Whitfield argues that this unpopularity is no accident of fashion: raw concrete streaks and stains as it weathers, and monumental scale dwarfs the human body. The hostility such buildings provoke, he concludes, is a predictable response to formal qualities inherent in the style itself.", "text2": "Urban researchers led by Sofia Andrade showed participants photographs of Brutalist civic buildings. Those who first read brief accounts of the buildings' origins—many were commissioned as postwar investments in public housing, libraries, and universities—rated the same facades markedly more favorably than participants given no background did. Favorability rose further when the photographs showed the buildings animated by everyday use. Andrade concludes that aesthetic judgments of the style are strikingly sensitive to the framing that accompanies an encounter with it."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Andrade (Text 2) most likely respond to Whitfield's argument in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By suggesting that the hostility Whitfield attributes to qualities inherent in the style may depend substantially on the framing through which viewers encounter the buildings", "why": "Andrade's participants rated identical facades more favorably after learning the buildings' origins or seeing them in use, so she would counter that judgments are 'strikingly sensitive to framing' rather than a predictable product of the buildings' formal qualities alone."}, {"text": "By noting that many Brutalist buildings were originally commissioned as postwar investments in public housing, libraries, and universities", "why": "Accurately restates a detail from Text 2, but that background is merely the content of Andrade's experimental manipulation; simply reciting it does not engage Whitfield's claim about why the style provokes hostility.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "By denying that the formal qualities Whitfield identifies play any role in viewers' judgments of Brutalist buildings", "why": "Overstates Text 2: showing that framing shifts ratings does not establish that exposed concrete and monumental scale contribute nothing to viewers' judgments, and Andrade makes no such absolute claim.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "By arguing that public dislike of Brutalist architecture has faded as the buildings' postwar origins have become more widely known", "why": "Text 2 reports an experimental manipulation of framing, not a historical trend; Andrade never claims that awareness of the buildings' origins has actually spread or that dislike has declined over time.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-ctc-a3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Psycholinguist Ana Villanueva, reviewing two decades of research on the \"bilingual advantage\"—the proposal that managing two languages strengthens the brain's executive functions—argues that the effect is genuine but easily obscured. It emerges most reliably, she notes, in studies whose tasks approximate the demands of real-world language switching and among speakers who alternate between their languages frequently in daily life. Null results, in her account, come disproportionately from experiments built around simplified laboratory tasks bearing little resemblance to those demands.", "text2": "A meta-analysis by cognitive scientist Tobias Feld aggregated more than 150 studies comparing bilinguals and monolinguals on executive-function tasks. Once the sample was corrected for publication bias—the tendency for positive findings to reach print more readily than null ones—the bilinguals' average advantage was statistically indistinguishable from zero. Feld concludes that claims of a general bilingual advantage are unwarranted, though he grants that standard laboratory measures may be too coarse to register subtle benefits arising only under particular linguistic demands."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?", "choices": [{"text": "Bilinguals reliably outperform monolinguals on executive-function tasks administered under typical laboratory conditions", "why": "Contradicts both texts: Villanueva says null results cluster in simplified laboratory experiments, and Feld's bias-corrected meta-analysis found an average advantage on such tasks that was indistinguishable from zero.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "An executive-function advantage is most likely to appear among bilinguals who alternate between their languages frequently in daily life", "why": "Only Villanueva makes this claim; Feld holds that claims of a general advantage are unwarranted and concedes only that measures might miss subtle benefits, without identifying which speakers would show them.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Standard laboratory measures are incapable of detecting genuine cognitive differences between groups of speakers", "why": "Overstates both authors' hedged positions: Feld says such measures 'may be too coarse' to register subtle benefits, and Villanueva faults the tasks as unrepresentative—neither claims the measures cannot detect any real differences.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Whether bilinguals display an advantage in executive function may depend on the conditions under which the advantage is measured", "why": "Villanueva holds that the effect 'emerges most reliably' when tasks approximate real switching demands, and Feld grants that laboratory measures 'may be too coarse to register subtle benefits arising only under particular linguistic demands'; both thus tie detection of the effect to measurement conditions."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-ctc-a4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "The flying buttress has long been presented as the emblem of Gothic structural rationalism. Architectural historian Helen Draycott argues that the buttressing systems of the great thirteenth-century cathedrals answered a strictly mechanical problem: as builders pursued taller vaults and larger windows, walls could no longer absorb the vaults' outward thrust, so support had to migrate outside the building. In her account, every element of these systems—the placement of each pier, the curvature of each arch—represents a direct engineering response, refined through trial and error toward ever greater efficiency.", "text2": "When structural engineer Marta Petrescu subjected laser scans of several thirteenth-century French cathedrals to computational load analysis, she found that a number of upper-tier flying buttresses carry negligible weight: models of the buildings remained stable when those members were removed. Some, moreover, are positioned where they can contribute little to countering the vaults' thrust. Petrescu proposes that masons multiplied buttresses partly for expressive reasons—a fabric of audacious height, visibly strained, demanded an equally dramatic display of support."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Petrescu (Text 2) most likely respond to Draycott's argument in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By denying that flying buttresses played a significant role in supporting the vaults of thirteenth-century cathedrals", "why": "Too sweeping: Petrescu found that only certain upper-tier members carry negligible weight; her analysis does not question the structural role of the buttressing systems as a whole.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "By arguing that a strictly mechanical explanation cannot account for every element of the buttressing systems Draycott describes", "why": "Petrescu's models remained stable with certain buttresses removed, and she proposes that masons multiplied them 'partly for expressive reasons'—directly qualifying Draycott's claim that every element represents a direct engineering response."}, {"text": "By suggesting that Gothic masons lacked the knowledge needed to judge which buttresses their buildings actually required", "why": "Text 2 attributes the structurally redundant members to expressive intent, not to error or ignorance, and offers no assessment of the masons' technical understanding.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By agreeing that buttressing systems were progressively refined through trial and error toward ever greater structural efficiency", "why": "False agreement: the load-bearing redundancy Petrescu documents cuts against, rather than confirms, a narrative of refinement toward pure structural efficiency.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ctc-b1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "In 2020, a team led by astronomer Jane Greaves reported an absorption signature in Venus's cloud deck matching phosphine, a gas that on rocky planets such as Earth is produced almost exclusively by microbial life. After modeling volcanic activity, lightning, photochemistry, and meteoritic delivery, the team concluded that [[u]]no known abiotic process on Venus can generate phosphine at the abundance observed[[/u]], leaving an aerial biosphere among the remaining possibilities.", "text2": "Venus's clouds—strongly acidic and not directly sampled since the 1980s—are among the least chemically characterized environments in the inner solar system. An argument from elimination is only as strong as the catalog of alternatives it eliminates, and for Venus that catalog is thin: reactions in droplets of concentrated sulfuric acid remain almost wholly unmodeled. Ruling out known pathways there therefore establishes far less than it would for Earth."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By agreeing that an aerial biosphere is the most economical interpretation of the signal until a specific abiotic source of phosphine is demonstrated", "why": "Text 2 argues that the elimination argument warrants less confidence in exotic conclusions, not more; nothing in it privileges a biological reading of the signal.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By objecting that phosphine could not persist long enough in Venus's acidic clouds to be detected, so the reported absorption signature was likely misidentified", "why": "Text 2 never questions the detection itself—its target is the inference drawn from it—and it says nothing about phosphine's stability or about misidentification.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By cautioning that eliminating only cataloged processes carries little weight for an environment so sparsely characterized that uncataloged abiotic pathways may well exist", "why": "Text 2 states that an elimination argument 'is only as strong as the catalog of alternatives it eliminates,' that Venus's catalog is 'thin,' and that sulfuric acid droplet chemistry is 'almost wholly unmodeled'—directly qualifying the force of the underlined conclusion."}, {"text": "By arguing that phosphine should not be treated as a promising biosignature on any planet, Earth included", "why": "Text 2 confines its skepticism to Venus's poorly characterized clouds and explicitly contrasts Venus with Earth ('far less than it would for Earth') rather than extending the doubt to all planets.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ctc-b2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Recurring slope lineae (RSL)—dark streaks that lengthen down certain Martian slopes each warm season and fade as temperatures drop—were widely attributed to seeping brines after their discovery in 2011. Proponents noted that spectra at some RSL sites indicate hydrated salts and that [[u]]the streaks' strict confinement to the warmest months is precisely the behavior transient trickles of salty liquid water would display[[/u]].", "text2": "Measuring the terrain at dozens of RSL sites, planetary geologist Colin Dundas and colleagues found that the streaks consistently end where slopes flatten to the characteristic angle at which flowing dry sand comes to rest—a boundary that liquid, which can creep across shallower ground, would not respect. And since seasonal warming can also set dry grains moving, for instance by lofting gas from just beneath the surface, wet and dry mechanisms predict the same calendar."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Dundas and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By countering that the streaks' seasonal timing is equally expected under a dry mechanism, so it is the slopes at which the streaks stop, not the calendar they follow, that distinguish between the two accounts", "why": "Text 2 states that 'wet and dry mechanisms predict the same calendar,' so the timing cannot discriminate, while the streaks' ending at dry sand's angle of rest—'a boundary that liquid...would not respect'—does."}, {"text": "By replying that the streaks' timing is indeed decisive evidence, but evidence of flowing dry sand rather than of brines", "why": "Text 2 treats the timing as unable to discriminate between mechanisms ('the same calendar'); it is the terminal slope angle, not the seasonal pattern, that favors dry flows.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By contending that the hydrated salts detected at some RSL sites were probably misidentified minerals, removing the strongest evidence for liquid water", "why": "Text 2 never disputes the spectral detections of salts; its challenge rests on terrain measurements and on the ambiguity of the seasonal pattern.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By accepting that brines produce the streaks while noting that water so salty would be a poor indicator of a habitable environment", "why": "Neither text raises habitability, and Text 2 does not accept the brine explanation—its measurements point away from liquid altogether.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-ctc-b3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "The following text is adapted from Thomas Hardy's 1878 novel The Return of the Native.\n\nTo recline upon the heath was to know that [[u]]everything around and underneath had remained unaltered since prehistoric times[[/u]]. The sea changed, the fields changed, the rivers, the villages, and the people changed, yet Egdon remained; civilization was its enemy, and its soil had worn the same antique brown dress since the beginning of vegetation.", "text2": "The timeless Egdon of Hardy's opening chapter has become a critical commonplace, yet the novel keeps a quieter ledger: its narrator also inventories the heath's ancient burial mounds, its half-effaced roads, and the turf- and furze-cutting by which generations have earned a living from it. The heath's permanence, I would argue, is not a fact about the terrain but a mood its beholders—narrator included—cast over ground everywhere marked by human hands."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By denying that visitors to the heath genuinely experience the sensation of permanence the narrator describes", "why": "Text 2 grants that beholders feel the permanence—calling it 'a mood its beholders...cast'—and disputes its source, not the reality of the experience.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By agreeing that the heath has resisted human interference and citing its ancient burial mounds as proof of that resistance", "why": "Text 2 cites the burial mounds to the opposite effect—as part of the human handiwork that marks the ground—so they cannot serve as proof of resistance to human interference.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By conceding that the heath appears unaltered but pointing to gradual natural changes in its vegetation that the narrator overlooks", "why": "Text 2's counterevidence consists of human traces (mounds, roads, turf- and furze-cutting), not natural ecological change, which it never mentions.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By suggesting that the unaltered condition the narrator attributes to the heath is better understood as a way of perceiving the landscape than as a property of it, given the durable human marks the novel elsewhere records", "why": "Text 2 argues the permanence is 'not a fact about the terrain but a mood its beholders...cast' over ground 'everywhere marked by human hands,' citing the novel's own inventory of mounds, roads, and turf-cutting."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-ctc-b4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "The orbits of the dozen or so most distant known trans-Neptunian objects (TNOs) cluster together in space, an alignment that astronomers Konstantin Batygin and Michael Brown calculate has under a 1 percent chance of arising randomly among the known objects. They propose that the gravity of an undiscovered planet several times Earth's mass is the likeliest shepherd, while granting that the census of such distant bodies is still sparse enough that new discoveries could shift the statistics.", "text2": "Telescopic surveys do not sample the sky evenly: weather, scheduling, and the Milky Way's glare concentrate discoveries of distant TNOs in particular directions, which can manufacture apparent orbital alignments. Astronomer Renata Vogel argues that once these biases are modeled, the evidence for clustering weakens markedly, and that only a survey with uniform, well-documented coverage—and the additional objects it would find—can show whether any alignment remains for a planet to explain."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, both Batygin and Brown (Text 1) and Vogel (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?", "choices": [{"text": "The apparent orbital clustering is chiefly a product of the uneven sky coverage of the surveys that discovered the distant objects.", "why": "Only Vogel entertains this, and even she frames it as a possibility that a uniform survey must test; Batygin and Brown maintain the clustering is unlikely to be random.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "The currently known sample of distant trans-Neptunian objects is too limited to put the question of the clustering's origin fully beyond dispute.", "why": "Text 1's researchers grant that the census is 'sparse enough that new discoveries could shift the statistics,' and Vogel holds that only a broader, uniformly conducted survey can settle whether any alignment needs explaining—so both treat the current sample as insufficient to close the question."}, {"text": "An undiscovered massive planet is the only mechanism capable of herding the distant objects' orbits into alignment.", "why": "Text 1 calls the planet the 'likeliest' shepherd, not the only conceivable one, and Vogel questions whether there is a genuine alignment to shepherd at all.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Statistical analysis of the objects found so far has established that the alignment cannot be the result of chance.", "why": "Vogel argues the reverse—modeling survey biases markedly weakens the statistical case—and even Batygin and Brown concede that new discoveries could shift the numbers.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ctc-c1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "New Caledonian crows fashion hooked twigs to pry grubs from tree crevices, and the precise shape of these tools differs from one region of the island to the next. Ethologist Marguerite Vasseur attributes this geographic variation to cumulative culture: [[u]]young birds acquire their manufacturing techniques by observing skilled elders, and useful refinements spread and persist only because each generation copies the one before it.[[/u]]", "text2": "To learn what the birds can do without a model to copy, biologist Ren Okafor hand-reared several juveniles in isolation, allowing them no opportunity to watch any other individual—crow or human—handle a stick. On reaching maturity, these birds spontaneously produced hooked tools closely resembling those of wild crows, though their tools lacked the subtle regional flourishes seen in free-living populations."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Okafor most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By maintaining that the geographic differences in tool shape must, like the tools themselves, be innate rather than passed between generations.", "why": "Reverses Text 2's own evidence: the isolated birds lacked the regional flourishes, which points toward those differences being socially transmitted, not innate.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By granting that crows learn to make tools by imitation but insisting that only the most talented individuals ever master the technique.", "why": "Concedes the imitation-based acquisition that Text 2 undercuts, and 'talent' among individuals is never mentioned in either text.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By observing that crows raised with no chance to watch anyone handle sticks still produce hooked tools, so the basic technique need not be acquired through imitation.", "why": "Text 2's isolated birds 'spontaneously produced hooked tools' with no model to copy, which directly undercuts Text 1's claim that the manufacturing technique is acquired by observing elders."}, {"text": "By proposing that the hand-reared crows must have modeled their tools on the humans who raised and fed them.", "why": "Contradicts Text 2's explicit condition that the birds had 'no opportunity to watch any other individual—crow or human—handle a stick.'", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ctc-c2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Economist Lucas Farrow argues that guaranteed-income programs, which pay households a fixed sum regardless of employment, inevitably shrink the labor force. Because recipients can cover their basic needs without a paycheck, he reasons, many will choose to work fewer hours or to stop working altogether, and policymakers should therefore expect the total supply of labor to fall wherever such programs are adopted.", "text2": "Evaluating a guaranteed-income pilot in a mid-sized city, economist Priya Nandal found that recipients worked roughly the same number of hours as comparable nonrecipients. What changed was the kind of work they did: recipients were likelier to quit poorly paid positions for jobs matching their skills, spending part of the money on the costs of job hunting, and they reported greater satisfaction with the positions they ultimately held."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Nandal most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By noting that recipients kept working about as much as nonrecipients and channeled the payments into finding better-matched jobs rather than into leaving the workforce.", "why": "Text 2 reports that hours worked were 'roughly the same' while recipients moved to jobs matching their skills, which contradicts Farrow's prediction that the labor supply will fall."}, {"text": "By contending that the payments prompt recipients to work well beyond the hours they previously did.", "why": "Overstates Text 2's finding in the opposite direction: hours were 'roughly the same,' not greater.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By accepting that recipients cut back their hours while arguing that the resulting improvements in job quality make the trade-off worthwhile.", "why": "Grants a reduction in hours that Text 2 specifically did not find, so it misrepresents the study's result.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By emphasizing that recipients reported greater satisfaction with their jobs, an outcome Farrow's argument fails to anticipate.", "why": "Accurately draws on Text 2, but job satisfaction does not address Farrow's claim, which is strictly about how much people work.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0}
);
